[[File:California's 14th congressional district (since 2027) (new version).svg|thumb|270px|California's 14th congressional district boundary from the 2026 elections<br/>{{maplink|from=California's 14th congressional district (2027–).map|text=Interactive map version}}]]
This seat was vacant following the resignation of Democrat [[Eric Swalwell]], who was re-elected with 67.8% of the vote in 2024.<ref name="HPVT"/> He chose not to run for reelection, instead running as a frontrunner in the [[2026 California gubernatorial election]] before suspending his campaign on April 12, 2026, following sexual assault allegations from multiple women.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Koseff |first1=Alexei |last2=Bollag |first2=Sophia |date=April 10, 2026 |title=Ex-staffer says Eric Swalwell, candidate for California governor, sexually assaulted her |url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/politics/article/eric-swalwell-allegations-22198271.php |access-date=April 13, 2026 |website=San Francisco Chronicle |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news |first1=Allison |last1=Gordon |first2=Isabelle |last2=Chapman |first3=Casey |last3=Tolan |first4=Pamela |last4=Brown |date=April 10, 2026 |title=Exclusive: Four women describe sexual misconduct by Rep. Eric Swalwell, including a former staffer who says he raped her |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/04/10/us/eric-swalwell-sexual-misconduct-allegations-invs |access-date=April 13, 2026 |publisher=CNN |language=en}}</ref> Following calls from fellow members of Congress, Swalwell resigned from Congress on April 14, 2026.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-13/democrat-swalwell-resigns-from-congress-after-sex-assault-claims?srnd=homepage-europe|title=Democrat Swalwell Resigns From Congress After Sex Assault Claims|last=Diaz|first=Alicia|date=April 13, 2026|access-date=April 13, 2026|website=[[Bloomberg News|Bloomberg]]}}</ref> 

The [[2026 California's 14th congressional district special election|special election]] to replace Swalwell was held on June 16, 2026, with a runoff round on August 18, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 14, 2026 |title=Governor Newsom issues proclamation setting special election for California Congressional District 14 |url=https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/04/14/governor-newsom-issues-proclamation-setting-special-election-for-california-congressional-district-14/ |access-date=April 14, 2026 |publisher=Governor of California |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kelley |first=Neal |title=Guide to Filling a Vacancy in Elective Office |url=https://ocvote.gov/election-library/docs/vacancy.pdf |access-date=April 14, 2026 |publisher=Orange County Registrar of Voters}}</ref> Wahab was declared the winner of the special election, and took office on September 2.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogerson |first=Riley |date=September 1, 2026 |title=Introducing Rep. Aisha Wahab |url=https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/09/02/congress/aisha-wahab-sworn-01061921 |access-date=September 3, 2026 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> She is the first [[Afghan American]] member of the [[United States Congress]].<ref>{{cite news |title= Progressive Aisha Wahab wins California special election to fill Eric Swalwell seat |first= Dani |last= Anguiano |website= The Guardian |date= August 20, 2026 |access-date= August 20, 2026 |url= https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/20/california-special-election-results-eric-swalwell}}</ref>

The regular primary election was still held on June 2, 2026. The regular general election will still be held on November 3, 2026.

